🔹 Problem

👉 Given:

array arr
integer k
integer threshold

👉 Count karo:

kitne subarrays (size = k) ka average ≥ threshold hai

🔹 Example
arr = [2,2,2,2,5,5,5,8]
k = 3
threshold = 4

👉 Check:

[2,2,2] → avg = 2 ❌
[2,2,2] → 2 ❌
[2,2,2] → 2 ❌
[2,2,5] → 3 ❌
[2,5,5] → 4 ✅
[5,5,5] → 5 ✅
[5,5,8] → 6 ✅

👉 Answer = 3

🔴 1. BRUTE FORCE
int numOfSubarrays(vector<int>& arr, int k, int threshold) {
    int n = arr.size();
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i <= n - k; i++) {
        int sum = 0;

        for (int j = i; j < i + k; j++) {
            sum += arr[j];
        }

        double avg = (double)sum / k;
        if (avg >= threshold) count++;
    }

    return count;
}
🔹 Complexity
O(n * k) ❌
🧠 Optimization Insight (VERY IMPORTANT)

👉 Condition:

avg >= threshold

👉 Multiply both sides by k:

sum >= k * threshold

🔥 HUGE OPTIMIZATION
👉 Ab division ki zarurat hi nahi!

🟢 2. OPTIMAL (Sliding Window)
int numOfSubarrays(vector<int>& arr, int k, int threshold) {
    int n = arr.size();
    int target = k * threshold;
    int sum = 0, count = 0;

    // first window
    for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) {
        sum += arr[i];
    }

    if (sum >= target) count++;

    // sliding window
    for (int i = k; i < n; i++) {
        sum += arr[i];       // add new
        sum -= arr[i - k];   // remove old

        if (sum >= target) count++;
    }

    return count;
}
